If the recorded junk flag at depth n for a bit-source is 0, then the two recorded indices name the two components of the generalized atom pair at that depth along the bit history of the bit-source.

The argument is induction on the depth. At zero the state stores the two master indices, which name the two masters, and that is the generalized atom pair at depth zero. At a successor the previous depth is already non-junk, so the inductive hypothesis identifies the previous indices. The two bits read from the remainder are the two components of the bit history. The outer flag being 0 forces the inner X sub-step to be non-junk. The invariant supplies the split preconditions, the X sub-step is correct for the first bit, and the Y sub-step is correct for the second bit against the specification of that X step. The successor equation assembles these into the two components at depth n + 1.
